.TH PYDEBUPREP 1 "October 2026" "pydebuprep 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pydebuprep \- read\-only health report for Debian and Ubuntu after a release upgrade
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pydebuprep
.RB [ report ]
.RB [ \-\-format\ \fIFORMAT\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-output\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR \ |\  \-\-output\ \fIFILE\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-baseline\ \fIFILE\fR \ |\  \-\-no\-baseline ]
.RB [ \-\-snapshot\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-checks\ \fIID,...\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-skip\ \fIID,...\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-min\-severity\ \fILEVEL\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.br
.B pydebuprep snapshot
.RB [ \-\-snapshot\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR ]
.br
.B pydebuprep list\-checks
.br
.B pydebuprep
.BR \-\-version \ |\  \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pydebuprep
inspects a Debian (12, 13) or Ubuntu (22.04, 24.04, 26.04) system after a
release upgrade and reports on APT sources, package state, configuration file
conflicts, the running kernel, DKMS modules, the bootloader, systemd units and
timers, the journal and kernel log, fstab and disk space.
.PP
It is strictly read\-only. It never installs, removes or reconfigures anything,
never runs
.BR "apt update" ,
and never restarts services. Each finding includes the commands to fix it, for
you to review and run yourself. Every external command it runs is checked
against a built\-in allowlist of read\-only commands.
.PP
Run it as root for complete results. Checks that need root are reported as
skipped otherwise.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B report
Run all checks and print a report. This is the default when no command is
given. If a snapshot exists, the newest one is used as a baseline and each
check reports what changed since it was taken.
.TP
.B snapshot
Record a baseline before upgrading: OS release, kernel, active services and
timers, APT sources, held packages, installed packages, mounts and DKMS modules.
Prints the path of the snapshot file.
.TP
.B list\-checks
List the available checks with their IDs and categories.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format:
.BR text " (default), " json ", " yaml ", " html " or " markdown .
All formats contain the same information in the same order.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Write the report to
.I DIR/pydebuprep\-HOSTNAME\-YYYYmmddTHHMMSSZ.EXT
instead of standard output. The directory must exist.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to
.IR FILE .
If it already exists, a UTC timestamp is inserted before the extension; an
existing file is never overwritten.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " FILE"
Compare against this snapshot instead of the newest one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-baseline
Do not compare against any snapshot.
.TP
.BI \-\-snapshot\-dir " DIR"
Where snapshots are stored and looked up (default
.IR /var/lib/pydebuprep/snapshots ).
.TP
.BI \-\-checks " ID,..."
Run only the listed checks.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " ID,..."
Do not run the listed checks.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-severity " LEVEL"
Hide findings below
.IR LEVEL :
.BR info " (default), " warning " or " critical .
The exit status still counts hidden findings.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never colorize text output. Color is also disabled when standard output is not
a terminal or the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is set.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No findings at warning level or above.
.TP
.B 1
The most severe finding is a warning.
.TP
.B 2
At least one critical finding.
.TP
.B 3
pydebuprep itself failed: invalid arguments, an unreadable baseline, or an
output file that could not be written.
.PP
Skipped checks do not affect the exit status.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/pydebuprep/snapshots/
Default snapshot directory (mode 0750). Snapshots are JSON files named after
the UTC time they were taken.
.SH EXAMPLES
Take a snapshot, upgrade, reboot, then report:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep snapshot
sudo apt full\-upgrade   # or do\-release\-upgrade
sudo reboot
sudo pydebuprep
.RE
.fi
.PP
Write an HTML report to /var/tmp:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep report \-\-format html \-\-output\-dir /var/tmp
.RE
.fi
.PP
Machine\-readable output for automation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep report \-\-format json \-\-no\-color > report.json; echo $?
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt (8),
.BR dpkg (1),
.BR needrestart (1),
.BR debsums (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR findmnt (8)
